.TH OMARCHYFAN 1 "2026" "omarchy-fan" "ThinkPad Fan Control"
.SH NAME
omarchyfan \- control a ThinkPad's fan from the Omarchy bar or a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B omarchyfan
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B omarchyfan quiet | balanced | performance
.br
.B omarchyfan manual
.I LEVEL
.br
.B omarchyfan follow on | off
.br
.B omarchyfan status | current | stats | doctor | help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B omarchyfan
is the command behind the ThinkPad Fan Control plugin for the Omarchy bar. The fan panel in the bar calls exactly
this command, so anything you can click there you can also do from a terminal.
.PP
The fan is driven by
.BR thinkfan (1).
.B omarchyfan
picks which thinkfan curve is active, or pins the fan at a fixed level. Changing the fan needs root, so each change is
sent to a small root-owned helper with
.BR pkexec (1).
A polkit rule lets a user at the local, active session do that without typing a password. Everything else here
only reads.
.PP
The command is a link in
.I ~/.local/bin
that the plugin creates the first time the fan widget starts in the bar. With no
.IR command ,
.B omarchyfan
runs
.BR status .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B quiet
Apply the Quiet curve. The fan stays off the longest and the CPU runs warmest.
.TP
.B balanced
Apply the Balanced curve. A smooth curve that is the default.
.TP
.B performance
Apply the Performance curve. The fan never switches fully off and spins up early, so the CPU runs cooler.
.TP
.BI manual " LEVEL"
Hold the fan at one fixed level, ignoring temperature.
.I LEVEL
is a whole number from 0 to 8: 0 is off, 1 to 7 are the ThinkPad's fan levels, and 8 is full speed. Anything else is
rejected with a message and exit status 1. The level is remembered, and the active profile becomes
.BR manual .
.TP
.B follow on | off
Choose whether a change of power profile also changes the fan (see
.BR "FOLLOWING THE POWER PROFILE" ).
Follow is on by default.
.TP
.B status
Print one line with the active profile, the CPU temperature in degrees C (the ThinkPad's own embedded controller
sensor), the fan speed in RPM and the setup state (see
.BR "SETUP STATES" ),
then the state of the
.B thinkfan
service on a second line (for example
.BR active ).
.TP
.B current
Print only the name of the active profile:
.BR quiet ,
.BR balanced ,
.B performance
or
.BR manual .
.TP
.B stats
Print the same facts as tab-separated
.I key value
lines (follow, manual, setup, profile, temp, rpm, level, service). The panel reads this; it is handy for scripts.
.TP
.B doctor
Print a read-only report on whether this machine is supported: the model and BIOS, whether the
.B thinkpad_acpi
driver is loaded with
.BR fan_control ,
the fan levels it offers, whether the fan speed and the first temperature slot (the one the curves follow) can be
read, whether there is a second fan, and whether
.B thinkfan
and the root helper are installed. Each line starts with
.BR ok ,
.B warn
or
.BR FAIL .
The exit status is 1 if any line is
.BR FAIL ,
otherwise 0. Paste the output into an issue when asking about a new model.
.TP
.B help | \-h | \-\-help
Show this page, or a short summary if the page is not installed.
.SH PROFILES
Each profile is a thinkfan curve. Temperatures are the ThinkPad's CPU sensor, in degrees C. Each fan level has a
temperature at which the fan moves up to the next level and a lower one at which it moves back down, so the fan
does not flutter around a threshold.
.TP
.B quiet
The fan stays off until about 65, and reaches full speed above about 92.
.TP
.B balanced
The fan stays off until about 56, and reaches full speed above about 85.
.TP
.B performance
The fan never turns off (level 1 at the very least), and reaches full speed above about 74.
.PP
The exact rows are in the
.I .yaml
files listed under
.BR FILES ,
one row per level as
.RI [ level ", " "lower temperature" ", " "upper temperature" ].
.SH FOLLOWING THE POWER PROFILE
While the fan widget is running in the bar, a small background process watches
.BR power\-profiles\-daemon .
When the power profile changes and follow is on, the fan profile changes with it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
power-saver   \->  quiet
balanced      \->  balanced
performance   \->  performance
.fi
.RE
.PP
It does not matter what changed the power profile: you, a script, or Omarchy itself when you plug in or unplug the
charger. With follow off, your fan setting stays until you change it yourself. A manual change always sticks until the
next power profile change (with follow on).
.SH SETUP STATES
The fourth item of
.B status
(and the
.B setup
line of
.BR stats )
tells you whether the fan can be controlled:
.TP
.B ready
Everything is in place.
.TP
.B unsupported
This is not a ThinkPad (there is no thinkpad_acpi driver). The widget hides itself.
.TP
.B no\-thinkfan
thinkfan is not installed. Press the panel's install button, or run
.IR install\-helper.sh .
.TP
.B no\-helper
The root helper package is not installed. Same remedy.
.TP
.B needs\-enable
The helper is there, but thinkfan is not running or the driver's
.B fan_control
option is off. Press the panel's enable button, or run
.BR "pkexec /usr/lib/omarchy\-fan/helper enable" .
.PP
Until it is ready, the fan stays on the laptop firmware's own control, which is safe.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B omarchyfan status
Where things stand right now.
.TP
.B omarchyfan balanced
Back to the default curve.
.TP
.B omarchyfan manual 4
Pin the fan at level 4.
.TP
.B omarchyfan manual 8
Full speed, for example before a heavy job.
.TP
.B omarchyfan follow off
Keep my fan setting even when the power profile changes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/omarchyfan
This command: a link to the plugin's
.IR bin/omarchyfan ,
made by the plugin. A real file with that name is never replaced. (The old name,
.BR omarchy\-fan ,
is kept as a legacy link, see
.BR "LEGACY NAME" .)
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/man/man1/omarchyfan.1
This page: a link to the plugin's
.IR man/omarchyfan.1 ,
made by the plugin in a directory that
.BR man (1)
searches by default.
.TP
.I ~/.config/omarchy/plugins/io.github.gunz0blazin.thinkpad\-fan/
The plugin: the panel, this command, and the follower that watches the power profile.
.TP
.I ~/.config/omarchy/fan/
Your choices:
.I active
(the active profile),
.I manual\-level
(the last manual level) and
.I no\-follow
(present when follow is off).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/omarchy\-fan/helper
The only part that runs as root. It accepts a profile name or a level, nothing else, and writes the thinkfan
configuration from the profiles below.
.TP
.I /usr/share/omarchy\-fan/profiles/{quiet,balanced,performance}.yaml
The three curves.
.TP
.I /etc/thinkfan.yaml
The configuration thinkfan is using right now. If you had your own before, the first copy is kept as
.IR /etc/thinkfan.yaml.omarchy\-fan.bak .
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit\-1/actions/org.omarchy.fan.policy
Lets a local, active session run the helper without a password.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/thinkfan.service.d/omarchy\-fan.conf
Lifts systemd's start-rate limit (moving the slider restarts thinkfan) and, just before thinkfan starts, reloads the
driver with
.B fan_control=1
if the early-boot load left it off.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command worked.
.TP
.B 1
A bad argument (for example a level above 8), or the change was refused or failed (for example the
.BR pkexec (1)
prompt was cancelled).
.SH UNINSTALL
.nf
omarchy plugin remove io.github.gunz0blazin.thinkpad\-fan
rm \-f ~/.local/bin/omarchyfan ~/.local/share/man/man1/omarchyfan.1
rm \-f ~/.local/bin/omarchy\-fan ~/.local/share/man/man1/omarchy\-fan.1
rm \-rf ~/.config/omarchy/fan
sudo systemctl disable \-\-now thinkfan
sudo pacman \-Rns omarchy\-fan\-helper $(pacman \-Qq thinkfan thinkfan\-debug 2>/dev/null)
sudo rm \-f /etc/thinkfan.yaml
rm \-rf ~/.cache/yay/thinkfan
.fi
.PP
That removes everything the plugin put on the system. The
.B pacman
line also removes
.B thinkfan
(and the
.B thinkfan\-debug
package the AUR build adds); delete the
.B $(pacman ...)
part if you used thinkfan before this plugin and want to keep it. If you had your own
.I /etc/thinkfan.yaml
before, the helper saved it as
.IR /etc/thinkfan.yaml.omarchy\-fan.bak :
move it back after the steps above.
.PP
Removing the plugin alone does not stop thinkfan: the fan keeps running on the last curve you chose until you disable
the service.
.SH LEGACY NAME
Before version 1.2 the command was called
.B omarchy\-fan
(with a hyphen). That name still works for now, so existing scripts and habits keep working: it is a small forwarder
that runs
.B omarchyfan
with the same arguments and gives the same results. When it is run in a terminal it also prints a notice to standard
error saying the name is old; scripts that capture its output are not affected. A matching, short manual page is
installed too (see
.BR omarchy\-fan (1)).
.PP
.B The old name will be removed in a future release.
Switch scripts to
.BR omarchyfan .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR thinkfan (1),
.BR thinkfan.yaml (5),
.BR powerprofilesctl (1),
.BR pkexec (1),
.BR polkit (8)
.PP
